BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Introduction to Chainmail Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261121T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261121T160000
UID:6aceb2eba76c9f3e2ae81290@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Chainmail\, the ancient art of linking metal rings together in
  a chain or mesh weave\, has a long history within cultural practices from
  all over the world. These days you might not need a piece of armour to pr
 otect yourself during a swordfight\, but you still might want to know how 
 to make some - whether for yourself or to deck out a favourite toy! Or\, 
 maybe you want to make some unique jewelry: bracelets\, necklaces\, or ea
 rrings. Why not both?\nRegistration for both sessions is required:\n1st se
 ssion\, Nov 7: Basics! Opening and closing rings\, Japanese 1-in-1\, 2-in
 -2\, or 3-in-3 chain weave\, attaching clasps. Make a bracelet or a short
  necklace.\n2nd session\, Nov 21: Pick 1 option - barrel or box chain we
 ave\, or 4-1 European mesh weave. Make a bracelet\, a short necklace\, o
 r a base for a larger piece.\nPlease note that chainmail requires repetiti
 ve hand motions\, gripping\, and close visual attention to detail.\nThese
  sessions are oriented towards beginners but more advanced learners are al
 so welcome.\nInformation Collection Notice: The personal information on th
 is form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Librar
 ies Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be
  used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questi
 ons about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answ
 erline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toro
 nto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Bagshaw Room\, 1st floor.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aceb2eba76c9f3e2ae81290
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
